    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Kerma culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerma.jpg" alt="Kerma" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kerma <span class="kid-tale-cr">Lassi, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 4,500 years ago, people built a walled village called Kerma, by the Nile in Sudan.</p><p>It grew into a big city, the capital of a strong Nubian kingdom.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cattle <span class="kid-tale-cr">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Most people farmed in villages beside their fields, and others herded goats, sheep and cattle.</p><p>Kings lay under huge mounds with thousands of cattle skulls, and people were killed to be buried with them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-deffufa.jpg" alt="Western Deffufa" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Western Deffufa <span class="kid-tale-cr">walter callens, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>In the city’s centre stood the Western Deffufa, a huge mudbrick temple.</p><p>They honoured the ram, the lion, and a hippo and a giraffe with wings.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senusret-i.jpg" alt="Senusret I" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Senusret I <span class="kid-tale-cr">W. M. Flinders Petrie (1853-1942), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Egypt, to the north, wanted Kerma’s gold, cattle, ivory, ebony and incense.</p><p>The two lands traded and fought, and Egypt’s king Senusret I built forts on the border.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg" alt="Nile" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nile <span class="kid-tale-cr">Fiktube, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 3,500 years ago, Kerma’s army of archers raided deep into Egypt.</p><p>Then an Egyptian king destroyed Kerma, but its people rebelled for about 200 years.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kushite-empire.jpg" alt="Kushite Empire" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kushite Empire <span class="kid-tale-cr">Matthias Gehricke, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Later, the kings of Kush honoured Kerma as part of their past.</p><p>Statues of these later kings, found near Kerma, are now shown in the Kerma Museum.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> grew from a fortified village into a city of about 25 hectares, the capital of a centralised Nubian kingdom that controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from the First to the Fourth Cataract.</li><li>Rich in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, it traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and fought it, raiding deep into Egypt around 1575–1550 BCE in alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> around 1504 BCE, but rebellions went on for two centuries, and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> honoured Kerma as part of its past.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Egyptian royal statues and monuments looted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>’s raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were carried back to Kerma, and the defeat is believed to have been erased later from Egypt’s official records; it is known from an inscription in the tomb of the governor Sobeknakht II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elkab">Nekheb</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

