    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 3500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture develops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-nubia">Upper Nubia</a>, with small kingdoms and settled farming</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A new fortified village at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> begins the Early Kerma period</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>’s Middle Kingdom builds forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> fortifies Sai as its border</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1750 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Classic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> period begins: the city grows to about 25 hectares and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a> is built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1575–1550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, invades deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1504 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920s</span><span class="rf-text">George Andrew Reisner excavates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, taking it for an Egyptian outpost</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">Charles Bonnet’s Swiss team finds a cache of granite statues of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kerma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerma.jpg" alt="Kerma" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerma_city.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lassi, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture takes its name from Kerma, a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in the part of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-nubia">Upper Nubia</a>. It was preceded by the pre-Kerma culture, which developed between about 3500 and 2500 BCE and formed statelets and small kingdoms, probably larger than those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-group-culture">A-Group culture</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>. The earliest settlement at Kerma, lying under the later cemetery, was a village of wooden buildings, with storage pits, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> pens, two small rectangular buildings and fifty round huts, ringed by a wooden palisade and earthen walls. Upper Nubia had contact with the Old Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and its early regional rulers seem at first to have been loyal to the Egyptian kings.</p><p>Archaeologists divide the Kerma culture by its pottery and other finds into periods that roughly match Egypt’s: Early Kerma (2500–2000 BCE), Middle Kerma (2000–1750 BCE) and Classic Kerma (1750–1500 BCE), followed by a Late or Post-Kerma phase under Egyptian rule. Over these centuries Kerma grew from a newly founded fortified village to a fortified town of 3 hectares and then a fully urban settlement of about 25 hectares.</p><p>Its people were mixed. Christian Simon’s study of the skeletons, as reported by Claude Rilly, found three main groups, one of which may descend from the pre-Kerma population that founded the new town 4 kilometres from the old settlement when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>’s bed shrank. A 2022 study of DNA from the hair of a farming and herding person at Kadruka, from about 2000 BCE, found them genetically indistinguishable from early pastoralists of Kenya and Tanzania, consistent with herders spreading south along the Nile.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cattle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bulgarian_gray_cattle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> was a sizeable kingdom: Egyptian records speak of its rich, populous farmland. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, it seems to have been highly centralised, and at its height it controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from the First to the Fourth Cataract, a domain as long as that of ancient Egypt. Most of its people lived in villages beside their fields, while some districts specialised in herding goats, sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, or in processing gold. Certain towns gathered farm produce and directed trade. Surveys have found many Kerma sites south of the capital along now-dry channels of the Nile, and work ahead of the Merowe Dam traced them upriver at least as far as the Abu Hamad and Mograt Island area.</p><p>Only Kerma itself, with Doukki Gel, and Sai Island seem to have had large urban populations. Both show wealthy elites and a class of officials who watched over goods arriving from distant lands and supervised shipments from administrative buildings. Farming in the region dates back to the pre-Kerma period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> working is attested at Kerma from about 2200–2000 BCE.</p><p>Cattle mattered greatly. The royal tombs held the skulls of thousands of cattle, some brought from far districts, presumably as tribute from rural communities when a king died, which parallels the later importance of cattle as royal property elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Kerma’s army was built around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. In the Classic period the kings were buried under great tumuli in the city’s cemetery, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a> and secondary burials; two of the largest mounds were decorated with white quartzite cones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-deffufa.jpg" alt="Western Deffufa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">walter callens, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> was quite different from the later Napatan and Meroitic traditions, which were shaped by the Egyptian gods brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-nubia">Upper Nubia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. In the Classic Kerma period animals were shown in their full natural form, without the human bodies of Egyptian gods. Four animals held the highest importance: the ram, the lion, the winged hippopotamus and the winged giraffe. Amulets were common, and some seem to show mythical creatures.</p><p>Some scholars believe the people of Kerma held an animist view of the world, pointing to their treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> as a sacred place, a tradition later taken up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> of the New Kingdom, who built a great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> there. The later Nubian lion god Apedamak is thought to have grown out of Kerma’s reverence for lions, which were given human form as gods only in the Meroitic period.</p><p>At the centre of the city stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a>, a large monumental temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> built in the Classic period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The periods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> are defined largely by its pottery, which archaeologists use to date sites and trace the culture’s spread. Its builders worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and earth at first, and later in fired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a>: during the Middle and Classic periods Egyptian influence shows in straight-walled mudbrick buildings, more elaborate burials and imported prestige goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a>, a massive mudbrick temple, and the great royal tumuli are its largest monuments.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> metallurgy is attested at Kerma from about 2200–2000 BCE, and gold was processed in parts of the kingdom. The animal imagery of the Classic period, rams, lions and winged hippopotami and giraffes, appears in its art and on amulets.</p><p>The language of Kerma is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-behrens">Peter Behrens</a> and Marianne Bechaus-Gerst argued from herding words borrowed into the Nubian language <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a> that Kerma’s people spoke a Cushitic, Afroasiatic language. Claude Rilly instead proposes an Eastern Sudanic language of the Nilo-Saharan family, possibly ancestral to Meroitic. Julien Cooper, studying place names and personal names in Egyptian texts, finds that names from Kush look Eastern Sudanic while those from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> look Afroasiatic, and suggests an Eastern Sudanic language was spoken at Kerma by at least 1800 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senusret-i.jpg" alt="Senusret I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Senusret_I_Petrie_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W. M. Flinders Petrie (1853-1942), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> stood between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the African interior and played an important role in passing luxury goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> to Egypt. Egypt coveted its gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, milk products, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. The Gash Group of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> was in contact with Kerma throughout its history, and its main site, Mahal Teglinos, became an important trading partner. By 2300 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-group-culture">C-Group culture</a> appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, probably from the Dongola Reach near Kerma. As Egypt’s Old Kingdom collapsed, Kerma expanded north to Aswan.</p><p>Under the Middle Kingdom, Egypt campaigned in Lower Nubia, possibly to check Kerma, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> built forts at Ikkur, Quban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniba">Aniba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a> and Kor to guard the border and the gold mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-allaqi">Wadi Allaqi</a>. Kerma strongly fortified Sai, its border with Lower Nubia. When Egyptian power weakened in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>, Kerma reached its height, absorbed the kingdom of Sai and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>. An inscription in the tomb of Sobeknakht II at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elkab">Nekheb</a> records that Kerma invaded deep into Egypt between about 1575 and 1550 BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> struck back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> led several campaigns south that destroyed Kerma and ended in Egypt’s annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> around 1504 BCE, with a frontier at Kanisah Kurgus beyond the Fourth Cataract. Rebellions continued for about 220 years, until around 1300 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kushite-empire.jpg" alt="Kushite Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rulers_of_Kush,_Kerma_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthias Gehricke, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Egyptian rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>’s culture became increasingly Egyptian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> was governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, the “King’s Son of Kush”, Egyptian towns were founded on Sai Island and at Sedeinga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soleb">Soleb</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirgissa">Mirgissa</a> and Sesibi, and major ceremonies were held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>. Around 1000 BCE, about five centuries after Kerma’s fall, the more Egyptianised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> emerged. How much it continued Kerma is hard to judge, but the early Kushite kings used Kerma for royal burials and ceremonies, their funerary compounds at Napata follow a similar plan, and caches of their statues were left at Kerma, suggesting that they saw a link with the old capital.</p><p>When George Andrew Reisner excavated Kerma in the 1920s, he took it for the base of an Egyptian governor whose successors became independent kings, because he found inscribed Egyptian statues in the great burials. From the mid-20th century excavations showed that the city was far larger and more complex, and that its material culture and burials were overwhelmingly local. The Swiss archaeologist Charles Bonnet was among the first to challenge Reisner, and it took twenty years for Egyptologists to accept his arguments.</p><p>In 2003 Bonnet’s team found near Kerma a cache of monumental black granite statues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tanutamun</a>, now shown in the Kerma Museum.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> culture was the civilisation of a Nubian kingdom centred on Kerma in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from about 2500 to 1500 BCE. It grew into a centralised state controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from the First to the Fourth Cataract, traded gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and fought it. Egypt conquered it around 1504 BCE, and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> kept a link with its old capital.</em></p>

